{% commandanatomy
   command="align"
   subcommand="split"
   input="--input concat.nex"
   inputSub="one alignment"
   option="--input-partition parts.nex"
   optionSub="partition file" /%}

SEGUL alignment splitting splits a concatenated alignment into multiple alignments based on an input partition.

To split an alignment, you need two input files: the concatenated alignment and the partition file. If you skip inputting the partition file, the app will assume it is embedded in the alignment file.

```bash
segul align split --input <path-to-alignment> --input-partition <path-to-partition-file>
```

For example:

```bash
segul align split --input concat-alignment.nexus --input-partition concat-alignment-partition.nex
```

## Check Partition for Errors

By default, SEGUL validates that:

1. Partition names are unique and all site positions fit within the alignment length.
2. Sites cover `1..=max_end` continuously with no overlaps or gaps (including across merged and strided ranges).

Use the `--skip-checking` flag to skip coverage and overlap checks. This is helpful if you only want to extract a subset of a concatenated alignment.

```bash
segul align split --input concat-alignment.nexus --input-partition concat-alignment-partition.nex --skip-checking
```

## Supported partition formats for splitting alignment

SEGUL supports standard, codon/strided (`\3`), merged multi-range, PartitionFinder, and IQ-TREE `charpartition` schemes in RAxML and NEXUS formats.

RAxML with datatype or substitution model labels:

```text
DNA, locus_1 = 1-5
DNA, locus_2 = 6-10
```

```text
GTR+I+G, Subset1 = 1-100, 201-300
HKY+G, Subset2 = 101-200
```

RAxML without datatype labels, or with codon strides:

```text
locus_1 = 1-5
locus_2 = 6-10
```

```text
DNA, locus_1_Subset1 = 1-300\3, 601-900\3
DNA, locus_1_Subset2 = 2-300\3, 602-900\3
```

NEXUS `sets` / `assumptions` blocks, including merged ranges, `[comments]`, multi-line definitions, and `charpartition` blocks (such as IQ-TREE `best_scheme.nex`):

```text
#nexus
begin sets;
  charset locus1 = 1-100;
  charset locus2 = 101-200;
  charset locus3 = 201-300;
  charpartition mymodels =
    GTR+F+I+G4: locus1 locus3,
    HKY+F+G4: locus2;
end;
```

When splitting merged or strided partitions, SEGUL sorts the selected site positions in ascending order so each output alignment preserves the original alignment column order.

The SEGUL partition parser tolerates extra spaces around numbers and separators, but forbids spaces inside locus names:

```text
#Nexus
begin sets;
  charset locus_1 = 1 - 5 ;
  charset locus_2 = 6 - 10 ;
end;
```

But this will not work (note the space inside the quotes between the locus and number):

```text
#Nexus
begin sets;
  charset 'locus 1' = 1-5;
  charset 'locus 2' = 6-10;
end;
```

Some programs write locus names with file extensions (which contain a dot). In that case, SEGUL replaces dots with underscores to avoid name conflicts when changing the file extension.

For example:

```text
#Nexus
begin sets;
  charset locus_1.nex = 1 - 5 ;
  charset locus_2.nex = 6 - 10 ;
end;
```

In the above case, SEGUL writes `locus_1_nex.nex` and `locus_2_nex.nex` (if saved to NEXUS).

## All options

| Option                     | Description                                                                                   |
| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `-i`, `--input`            | Concatenated alignment. Required                                                              |
| `-f`, `--input-format`     | Input format: `auto` (default), `fasta`, `nexus`, or `phylip`                                 |
| `--datatype`               | Data type: `dna` (default), `aa`, or `ignore`                                                 |
| `-I`, `--input-partition`  | Partition file. Without it, SEGUL reads the partition embedded in the alignment               |
| `-p`, `--partition-format` | Format of the partition file: `charset`, `nexus`, or `raxml` (auto-inferred when omitted)     |
| `--skip-checking`          | Skip checking partition coverage and overlaps                                                 |
| `-F`, `--output-format`    | Output format: `nexus` (default), `phylip`, `fasta`, `nexus-int`, `phylip-int`, or `fasta-int` |
| `-o`, `--output`           | Output directory. Default: `Align-Split`                                                      |
| `--prefix`                 | Prefix of the output files                                                                    |
| `--force`                  | Overwrite an existing output directory                                                        |
| `--log`                    | Log file path. Default: `segul.log`                                                           |
